# Env
.env
.env.local
# The files this product writes beside itself, each holding a key, none of them matched by the
# patterns above. Running init or serve inside a checkout is an ordinary thing to do while trying
# it out, and every one of these would then be sitting in a working tree waiting for git add -A.
#
# switchtender.env holds the master encryption key and its salt: with those two values every stored
# credential and every backup can be decrypted.
#
# producer-key.json is the identity that signs this install's audit bundles. Published, it lets
# anybody mint a receipt that verifies as this install, which is the one thing the chain exists to
# make impossible.
#
# witness-key.json is the witness's own signing key, and the whole value of a witness signature is
# that it is not the watched party's.
switchtender.env
producer-key.json
witness-key.json
.env.*.local
*.pem
*.key

# Python bytecode from the Ansible callback plugin. internal/roundhouse/plugins/switchtender.py is
# executed by ansible-playbook on every run the test suite performs, so the interpreter writes a
# __pycache__ beside it that regenerates constantly and belongs to nobody.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Local config
.claude/

# Wrangler (Cloudflare Pages CLI) local cache
.wrangler/

# Built binaries
/switchtender
/sitegen
/bench
/embedded-witness

# Internal campaign hold records, never published.
campaigns/

# Built at deploy from the LoomSeal the module pins, never committed. A committed verifier is a
# promise that somebody remembered to rebuild it, and site/verify shipped one five releases behind
# for exactly that reason.
site/verify/loomseal.wasm
site/verify/wasm_exec.js
